# Plot Twist: Docker Hub Works Fine — So Why Is the Pull Failing?

This result **rules out** network/firewall/proxy/DNS/TLS as the issue. Look at what we confirmed:

- ✅ DNS resolves (`index.docker.io` → 18.233.78.24)
- ✅ IPv4 connects cleanly (TLS 1.3 handshake completed)
- ✅ Certificate is legitimate Amazon-issued `*.docker.com` (no TLS interception)
- ✅ Docker Hub responds with 301, rate-limit headers intact
- ✅ `x-ratelimit-remaining: 179` — you're not rate-limited
- ❌ IPv6 addresses fail ("Network is unreachable") — **this is the smoking gun**

## The Real Root Cause: IPv6 First, IPv4 Broken Fallback

The `rancher-system-agent`'s embedded container pull library (it uses `go-containerregistry` under the hood) resolves DNS, gets **both IPv6 and IPv4 addresses**, and tries **IPv6 first**. IPv6 returns "Network is unreachable" instantly on this VM. Unlike `curl`, which cleanly fails over to IPv4, the Go-based image puller **doesn't reliably fall back** in many versions — it treats the immediate fail as a pull failure and bails. One second later, the agent tries `run.sh` against the empty directory and gets exit 127.

Your `curl` works because curl's Happy Eyeballs implementation fails over in ~300ms. The agent's image pull does not.

## Confirm It

```bash
# 1. Check IPv6 state
ip -6 addr
ip -6 route
cat /proc/sys/net/ipv6/conf/all/disable_ipv6
sysctl net.ipv6.conf.all.disable_ipv6

# 2. Force IPv4 for a test pull
curl -v -4 https://index.docker.io/v2/   # works
curl -v -6 https://index.docker.io/v2/   # should fail the same way as the agent

# 3. Verify the agent had IPv6 enabled when it ran
getent ahosts index.docker.io
```

If `ip -6 addr` shows addresses but `ip -6 route` shows no default route (or the default points nowhere reachable), you have **IPv6 enabled on the interface but no IPv6 transit** — classic edge/corporate network state. This is the exact failure mode.

## The Fix — Three Options, Pick One

### Option 1: Disable IPv6 on this VM (quickest fix)

```bash
# Runtime
sysctl -w net.ipv6.conf.all.disable_ipv6=1
sysctl -w net.ipv6.conf.default.disable_ipv6=1

# Persistent
cat > /etc/sysctl.d/99-disable-ipv6.conf <<EOF
net.ipv6.conf.all.disable_ipv6=1
net.ipv6.conf.default.disable_ipv6=1
net.ipv6.conf.lo.disable_ipv6=1
EOF
sysctl -p /etc/sysctl.d/99-disable-ipv6.conf

# Force the agent to retry
rm -rf /var/lib/rancher/agent/applied/*
systemctl restart rancher-system-agent
journalctl -u rancher-system-agent -f
```

This is the **right** fix if IPv6 isn't intended to work at this site. K3s itself runs fine on IPv4-only.

### Option 2: Force IPv4 at the DNS resolver level (more surgical)

Add the Docker Hub hosts explicitly so only IPv4 is returned:

```bash
cat >> /etc/hosts <<EOF
18.233.78.24    index.docker.io
18.233.78.24    registry-1.docker.io
18.233.78.24    auth.docker.io
18.233.78.24    production.cloudflare.docker.com
EOF
```

Not great because those IPs rotate, but proves the theory.

### Option 3: Fix IPv6 properly (if you actually need dual-stack)

Work with your network team to establish IPv6 transit at the site, or remove the IPv6 addresses that have no route:

```bash
ip -6 addr flush scope global dev eth0
# Or disable RA (router advertisement) acceptance on the NIC
echo 0 > /proc/sys/net/ipv6/conf/eth0/accept_ra
```

